grammar.notes:
* In Spanish, unlike in Portuguese, the third-person stressed pronouns, él, ella, ellos, and ellas, when used as a subject should only be used to refer to people and not things. This means that sentences like the following are grammatically incorrect:
*: {{uxa|es|Me gusta el español; él es muy bonito.|I like Spanish; it is very nice|q=grammatically incorrect}}
* The pronoun should either be dropped, or replaced with a demonstrative pronoun like este, depending on the context. An exception to this rule is when the pronoun is modified by an adjective, adposition, or phrase that may necessitate an explicit pronoun:
*: {{uxa|es|Nos encantó el sofá, pero ocuparía él solito toda nuestra sala.|We loved the couch, but it would occupy our entire living room by itself.}}
*: {{uxa|es|Leí las instrucciones, pero aún tengo dudas sobre ellas.|I read the instructions, but I still have questions about them.}}
* The stressed pronoun should not be used in cases where there is a duplicated pronoun:
*: {{uxa|es|El coche, ya lo encontraron a él?|The car, did you all already find it?|q=grammatically incorrect}}

According to the Royal Spanish Academy (RAE), there are four grammatically correct ways to say "to forget" using olvidar; namely, the verb can be:
* transitive;
* transitive and pronominal (this form is mostly used with the meaning "to forget" as in "to accidentally leave something behind"; for other meanings, such as "to fail to remember something" or "to deliberately not do something",  the RAE discourages its use, though it remains common in colloquial speech);
* intransitive and pronominal, with preposition de;
* pronominal and with the subject and object seemingly "swapped" (similarly to verbs like gustar or encantar); i.e., the thing that is forgotten is the subject, while the one who forgets is the indirect object. In this structure, a more accurate translation for olvidar into English is "to elude".

Hence, all of the below sentences are equally valid grammatically:
:(Ella) olvidó las llaves. / (Ella) se olvidó las llaves. / (Ella) se olvidó de las llaves. / (A ella) se le olvidaron las llaves. ― She forgot the keys.
:(Yo) siempre olvido llamarte. / (Yo) siempre me olvido llamarte.† / (Yo) siempre me olvido de llamarte. / (A mí) siempre se me olvida llamarte. ― I always …

* The indefinite article is not used:
*: {{coi|es|mil pesos|one thousand pesos}}
* When pluralized as a specific number, the form mil is still used:
*: {{coi|es|dos mil pesos|two thousand pesos}}
*: {{coi|es|cien mil pesos|one hundred thousand pesos}}

*The word cien is an apocopation of ciento. These two forms are generally not interchangeable; their use can be determined by the following rules:
** cien is used:
*** when not followed by other numerals: i.e., with the number "100" itself and numbers that end in "-100" in their numerical form (both as a quantity and as a standalone numeral):
***: {{coi|es|cien personas|one hundred people}}
***: {{coi|es|dos mil cien personas|two thousand one hundred people}}
***: {{coi|es|contar hasta el cien|to count up to one hundred}}
***: noventa y ocho... noventa y nueve... ¡cien! ― ninety-eight... ninety-nine... one hundred!
*** when immediately followed by a higher numeral to indicate a number with higher order of magnitude:
***: {{coi|es|cien mil|one hundred thousand}}
***: {{coi|es|cien mil uno|one hundred thousand and one}}
***: {{coi|es|cien millones|one hundred million}}
** ciento is used:
*** when immediately followed by a lower numeral to indicate a number with more significant figures:
***: {{coi|es|ciento dos|one hundred and two}}
***: {{coi|es|ciento cincuenta y ocho|one hundred and fifty-eight}}
***: {{coi|es|ciento cincuenta y ocho mil|one hundred and fifty-eight thousand}}
*…
